; A GPU-dispatched closure captures a *local* runtime-sized array, and the
; GPU reads it. The tree-walking runtime + real GPU already handle this:
; the scope struct's dynamic capture lands as its final member, which WGSL
; legally permits for storage-bound structs, so the scope binding
; uploads/validates/executes fine. The VM runtime is the gap (it can't
; slot-size a scope global with a runtime-sized field). Two follow-up
; constraints this pins: the planned "no runtime-sized struct fields on
; GPU" validation must not ban storage-bound scope structs with a single
; *trailing* dynamic capture (that would regress this), and multiple
; dynamic captures will need splitting into separate storage bindings,
; since WGSL allows only one trailing runtime-sized member.
@[storage-write 0 0]
(var out: [1: f32])

@cpu
(defn main []
  (let [weights (into-dynamic-array [1. 2. 3.])]
    (dispatch-compute-shader (fn []
                                 (= (out 0u)
                                    (+ (weights 0u) (weights 2u))))
                             (vec3u 1u))
    (print (out 0u))))
